Distribution
Rumex sanguineus is common in most of Europe south of about 60 degrees north, but not in Russia and rarely in the Mediterranean. It inhabits damp, shady places, especially in woodland. It is an agricultural weed that can be found in the vegetation surrounding arable fields.{{Citation needed|date=May 2021|reason= This ref is about species-poor weed assemblies in Neolithic agriculture, and describes this perennial plant as an annual. Can it really be referring to the same species? Either way need a ref for it being a crop weed in modern habitats}}
Rumex sanguineus is not native to North America with most reports of Rumex sanguineus being confused with R. conglomeratus or immature R. obtusifolius.
